<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hilandar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1198">1198</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Chilandar, Hilandar Monastery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hilandar is a Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, founded in 1198 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-nemanja">Stefan Nemanja</a>, then the monk Simeon, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. Richly endowed by Serbian rulers, it became the leading centre of Serbian religious life and literature in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and holds a great library of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and icons. It is one of the twenty ruling monasteries of Athos.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hilandar.jpg" alt="Hilandar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Саша Шљукић · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_the_Hilandar_monastery,_Mount_Athos,_Greece.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hilandar is the only Serbian Orthodox monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. The name is probably a Serbian form of the Greek chelandion, a type of Byzantine transport ship. In 1198 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III Angelos</a> issued a chrysobull giving the ancient monastery Helandaris to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>. Stefan Nemanja, a former Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> who had taken monastic vows as Simeon, joined his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>, who later became the first Archbishop of Serbia. Together they built the main church, dedicated to the Theotokos, between 1198 and 1200, and Sava issued a Typikon in Serbian. Simeon died there on 13 February 1200, and his remains were moved to Studenica in 1208.</p><p>After the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> occupation and was pillaged. Hilandar was among only 35 of some 300 monasteries to survive the Catalan Grand Company's raids of the early 14th century. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-milutin">Stefan Milutin</a> rebuilt and expanded it, completely reconstructing the main church in 1320. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>, when all of Athos was under his power, it enjoyed its highest prosperity; since his reign Hilandar has owned one fifth of Athos. After a fire in 1722 Bulgarian monks dominated until the late 19th century, and a Serbian presence returned in 1902.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Hilandar houses about 45 working monks and treats the Mother of God through her icon of the Three Hands as its abbess. It holds some 1,200 Slavic manuscripts, 172 Greek and 154 Serbian medieval documents, the icon of the Three Hands, said to have come from John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>'s sword. Its scriptorium developed the Serbian variant of Old Church Slavonic. In 1990 it was converted from an idiorrhythmic monastery into a cenobitic one. A fire on 4 March 2004 destroyed much of the walled complex and all its wooden elements, but the library and the historic icons were saved.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hilandar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…at the Dionysiou Monastery, a gift of the Emperor Alexios III Komnenos, which tradition says Patriarch Sergius carried around the walls of Constantinople in 626; at <b>Hilandar</b>, where the icon was untouched by a fire in 1837 while the monks chanted the Akathist before it; and at Zographou.</blockquote><a href="/songs/akathist">Akathist Hymn</a></li><li><blockquote>His youngest son, Rastko, became the monk Sava and helped establish Serbian Orthodox monasticism at <b>Hilandar</b> on Mount Athos.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-principality-of-serbia">Grand Principality of Serbia</a></li><li><blockquote>Twenty ruling monasteries, Greek, Serbian (<b>Hilandar</b>), Russian (Saint Panteleimon), Bulgarian (Zograf) and others, own the land and send representatives to the Holy Community at Karyes.</blockquote><a href="/state/monastic-community-of-mount-athos">Monastic Community of Mount Athos</a></li></ul></section>
